## Session 20 summary — major breakthrough; criterion NOT yet met

**The core blocker that stalled ~19 sessions is resolved.** I discovered the "mht tradeoff" (tcp_dlm_scaling needs low hold-time, dir_reuse needs high) was never actually one knob — and found a **principled fix: format-gated MHT**. A new param `mxfs_dir_sf_mht_ms=100` gives **shortform** dirs (tcp_dlm_scaling's 1-file churn dir — data inline, coherent via whole-inode reload) a short hold, while **leaf/btree** dirs (dir_reuse's 800-entry dir) keep `inode_mht_ms=300`. Result: **tcp_dlm_scaling 8/tcp went from reliably-failing (126s) to PASS 8/8 (39s)**.

**Verified results (build `4E047A49`):**
- **1/tcp = 16/16, 2/tcp = 17/17** (pre-gate build; need re-verify on new build)
- **4/tcp = 16/17**, **8/tcp = 16/17** — the core mht tradeoff resolved; all coherency tests + tcp_dlm_scaling pass

**Three residuals, all traced to root cause (all deep 8/4-node flaky perf/coherency, NOT the old blocker):**
1. **dir_reuse** — 8-node in-suite **timeout** (348s vs 300s; passes at 420s & at 4 nodes). Speed-bound by per-handoff drain cost.
2. **dlm_scaling** — 8-node window flake (7/8 once, 8/8 once).
3. **tcp_dlm_scaling rename-miss** — at sf_mht=100, `mxfs_dir_rebase_shortform` reloads the shared shortform dir from disk on a peer-driven gen advance and **clobbers the node's own committed-not-yet-checkpointed create** → RENAME-REVALIDATE-MISS (4/tcp test2 = 0 rounds).

**Ended with an UNTESTED targeted fix** (build `DCC2B8FD`, builds clean): added a guard in `mxfs_dir_rebase_shortform` to **skip the disk-reload while the dir inode holds un-checkpointed own work** (in-AIL/pinned/dirty) — preventing the own-create clobber while preserving the sess49 clean-cacher rebase. **Next session: deploy DCC2B8FD, repeat-test 4/8-tcp tcp_dlm_scaling for the rename-miss, confirm no coherency regression, then tackle dir_reuse speed + dlm_scaling flake.**

12 detailed ccmemory entries banked + CLAUDE.md head updated. The criterion (1/2/4/8 tcp 100%) is not met — I have not written the marker.

